# 11. `git add` vs `git commit`

| `git add`                     | `git commit`            |
| ----------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Moves changes to staging area | Saves staged changes    |
| Does not create commit        | Creates a commit        |
| Prepares changes              | Records changes         |
| Can stage selected files      | Records staged snapshot |

### Example

```bash
git add git-commands.md

git commit -m "update git commands"
```

---

# 12. Staging Area

The staging area allows me to select exactly which changes should be included in the next commit.

### Git Architecture

```text
┌─────────────────────┐
│   Working Directory │
│                     │
│  Modified Files     │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
        git add
           ↓
┌─────────────────────┐
│    Staging Area     │
│                     │
│ Selected Changes    │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           │
       git commit
           ↓
┌─────────────────────┐
│     Repository      │
│                     │
│  Commit History     │
└─────────────────────┘
```

---

# 13. Working Directory vs Staging Area vs Repository

| Area              | What it contains                 | Main command |
| ----------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------ |
| Working Directory | Files being created/modified     | Edit files   |
| Staging Area      | Changes selected for next commit | `git add`    |
| Repository        | Committed project history        | `git commit` |

### Easy Way to Remember

```text
Working Directory
       │
       │ git add
       ↓
Staging Area
       │
       │ git commit
       ↓
Repository
```

